WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
Tier three.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:06.186
These are the rare skills that get you hired over fifty other candidates.

3
00:00:06.186 --> 00:00:08.300
Having just one makes you memorable.

4
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.133
Here is the rule for this tier.

5
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:12.747
Pick one, and go deep.

6
00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:14.957
Spreading thin makes you forgettable.

7
00:00:14.957 --> 00:00:17.167
This is your specialization decision.

8
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:19.918
Here are your six options.

9
00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:24.633
Each one is rare enough to stand out when the hiring team sits down to debrief.

10
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:28.321
Evaluation pipelines built from scratch.

11
00:00:28.321 --> 00:00:30.099
A multi-model router.

12
00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:31.793
Agent observability.

13
00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:35.350
Speculative decoding and custom inference.

14
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.636
RLHF or DPO implementation.

15
00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:40.600
And on-device, edge LLM deployment.

16
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.441
Take evaluation pipelines.

17
00:00:43.441 --> 00:00:44.767
Why are they so rare?

18
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:47.500
Most candidates copy the RAGAS defaults.

19
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.300
Very few design domain-specific metrics and defend them.

20
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.133
That is the signal that someone actually shipped LLM systems.

21
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:58.500
The others are rare for different reasons.

22
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.800
A router needs quality tradeoffs, cost math, and the eval discipline to prove routing did not hurt.

23
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.271
Agent observability is nascent.

24
00:01:07.271 --> 00:01:12.033
You track trajectory accuracy, tool call accuracy and cost per task.

25
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:19.167
And on-device deployment, with llama cpp and quantization, has exploding demand and tiny supply.

26
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:21.762
You need exactly one.

27
00:01:21.762 --> 00:01:27.807
Speculative decoding, the deep systems option, is the highest paid combination that exists.

28
00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:29.667
But one done well is enough.

29
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.214
You need exactly one.

30
00:01:32.214 --> 00:01:39.592
Speculative decoding and custom inference is the deep systems option, and the highest paid combination that exists.

31
00:01:39.592 --> 00:01:42.800
But a single differentiator, done well, is enough.

32
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.500
Pick one and go deep.

33
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.800
Evals built from scratch signal that you really shipped.

34
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.128
And systems depth pays the most.

35
00:01:50.128 --> 00:01:53.867
Next up: real job description language, and how to read it.
